  "title": "A loopback caller can claim to be a trusted proxy and skip the token check",
  "detail": "The token check is skipped when `access === \"proxy\"`, and `classifyRequest` (src/server/access.ts:27) returns \"proxy\" for any peer in `trusted_proxies` that sends an `x-forwarded-*` header. When the proxy runs on the same machine (`trusted_proxies = [\"127.0.0.1\"]`), every loopback process can send those headers. Reproduced with a temporary probe: enforcement on, `trustedProxies = [\"127.0.0.1\"]`, `auth = \"proxy\"`, and a spoofed `x-forwarded-for` plus a public Host and Origin. The response was 201 and a run was created with no credentials. With `auth = \"required\"` the same request got 401. Host and Origin are caller-controlled. Fix: forwarded headers from a loopback peer must never replace the token or session requirement. Either require a configured proxy secret header on proxied requests from loopback, or refuse at config load the combination of enforcement, a loopback trusted proxy and `auth = \"proxy\"`, with a clear message. Test: the request above gets 401, and a correctly configured proxy still works."
