  "title": "A failed second start replaces the running daemon's token file",
  "detail": "`createApiToken` writes and renames the token file before `startHttp` knows it can bind. Reproduced with a temporary probe: with one daemon running, a second start with an injected bind failure rewrote the token file. The running daemon still accepted its original token (200) and refused the newly published one (401), so every client that re-reads the file (CLI, MCP, deploy, the gate-slot client the land queue depends on) was locked out until a restart. An accidental second `limitless serve` is enough to trigger it. Fix: publish the new token only once this process owns the listener, for example by binding first and then rotating. Or never replace a file that a live daemon is serving. Test: the sequence above leaves the running daemon's clients working."
